**HTML Form Submission Example**
=====================================

Below is a simple example of an HTML form that submits data to a server-side endpoint.

```html
<!-- index.html -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Form Submission Example</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Form Submission Example</h1>
    <form id="myForm" action="/submit" method="post">
        <label for="name">Name:</label>
        <input type="text" id="name" name="name" required><br><br>
        <label for="email">Email:</label>
        <input type="email" id="email" name="email" required><br><br>
        <input type="submit" value="Submit">
    </form>

    <script>
        // Add event listener to form submission
        const form = document.getElementById('myForm');
        form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
            // Prevent default form submission behavior
            e.preventDefault();
            // Get form data
            const formData = new FormData(form);
            // Submit form data to server-side endpoint
            fetch('/submit', {
                method: 'POST',
                body: formData
            })
            .then((response) => response.json())
            .then((data) => console.log(data))
            .catch((error) => console.error(error));
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Explanation:**

1. We create a simple HTML form with input fields for `name` and `email`, and a submit button.
2. We add an event listener to the form submission using JavaScript.
3. When the form is submitted, we prevent the default form submission behavior using `e.preventDefault()`.
4. We get the form data using the `FormData` API.
5. We submit the form data to a server-side endpoint using the `fetch` API.
6. We handle the response data from the server-side endpoint and log it to the console.

**Server-Side Endpoint:**

To handle the form submission data on the server-side, you would need to create an endpoint that accepts POST requests. For example, using Node.js and Express.js:

```javascript
// server.js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const port = 3000;

app.use(express.json());
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true }));

app.post('/submit', (req, res) => {
    const { name, email } = req.body;
    console.log(`Name: ${name}, Email: ${email}`);
    res.json({ message: 'Form submitted successfully' });
});

app.listen(port, () => {
    console.log(`Server listening on port ${port}`);
});
```

This is just a basic example, and you may need to modify it to fit your specific use case.

